# Tests that `cargo xtask stress` leaves out.
#
# An entry is a reason, written as comment lines, followed by one or more lines of a step
# and a test: `suites` with a file of an upstream suite (`ruby/test_x.rb`), one of its test
# methods (`ruby/test_x.rb#test_y`) or `sample`; `binding` or `guest` with the full name of
# a test as `cargo test` prints it; `suites-1.9.2` with a file of `test/ruby` of Ruby 1.9.2
# (`ruby/test_x.rb`) or one of its test methods, which the stressed tests of
# `vkrgss-ruby192-sys` read from here; `binding-1.9.2` with the full name of a test of
# `vkrgss-binding-rgss3` as `cargo test` prints it (`t::<name>` for a test of a file of
# `vkrgss-binding`); `guest-1.9.2` with the full name of a test of `rgss3.rs` of `vkrgss` or of
# the `guest_record.rs` files. Entries are separated by blank lines, and an entry without a reason fails
# a test. A value lost under stress is never a reason: it is a fault and is fixed.

# The collector stress of Ruby 1.9.2 (`GC.stress = true`), step `suites-1.9.2`: these files
# did not finish in 14 minutes under stress in the feasibility study of 2026-10-13
# (combinatorial tests) and are unexamined there. No stressed run of `vkrgss-ruby192-sys`
# includes them, neither that of the workspace tests nor the step.
suites-1.9.2 ruby/test_integer_comb.rb
suites-1.9.2 ruby/test_m17n_comb.rb
suites-1.9.2 ruby/test_yield.rb

# The collector stress of Ruby 1.9.2, step `suites-1.9.2` and the stressed run of the
# workspace tests: `test_status_and_stop_p` kills a sleeping thread and reads its state at
# once, `["aborting", false]`. Under `GC.stress = true` a tick of the timer thread can come
# between the two calls and let the killed thread run, which then reads as stopped; it
# failed so in two runs of the merge gate on 2026-10-13 while other work loaded the machine,
# once with the C source of `main`, and passed alone. A moment of scheduling, no lost value;
# the file runs it without the stress.
suites-1.9.2 ruby/test_thread.rb#test_status_and_stop_p
